it's been six years since the financial  crisis and then a lot of people who  wonder why does no Bank been brought up  on any criminal charges well in daiting  a bank is a tricky thing if the  government was to label a major  financial institution of criminal  enterprise there could then be a run on  that back causing hard greater damage to  the economy then whatever malfeasance  was conducted by that hey so what's a  federal prosecutor to do so far  government lawyers have avoided this  doomsday scenario by brokering  settlements called deferred prosecution  agreements in 2012 the US Department of  Justice accused bank HSBC of stunning  failures related to money laundering but  instead of going to court HSBC admitted  to the violations and agreed to forfeit  1.3 billion dollars today to big banks  are under the federal microscope Swiss  banking giant Credit Suisse is facing  indictment over helping Americans evade  taxes france's largest bank BNP Paribas  has been investigated for processing  transactions from blacklisted companies  in countries like Sudan Cuba and Iran  there haven't been any criminal  indictments yet but that doesn't mean  there won't be to be clearly no  individual or company no matter how  large or how profitable is above the law  the prevailing argument particularly in  the financial community is that any  criminal prosecution could result in a  bank losing its charter which could then  set off a panic in the market and cause  general financial havoc the US Attorney  for the Southern District of New York  Preet Bharara thinks that argument is  just a scare tactic calling it a gaping  liability loophole that blameworthy  companies are only too willing to  exploit the question is whether it's  just that or if banks that are too big  to fail are in fact too big to jail  you
 

